Auto Retry DB Connection:
    Demonstrates how to automatically retry database connection attempts.
    Uses decorators to wrap the connection function with retry logic.
    Retries up to 3 times if the database is not reachable.
    Logs warnings on each failure and error if all retries fail.
    Helpful in real-world applications where databases may be temporarily unavailable.
    Prevents app crashes due to transient DB issues.
    Uses functools.wraps for preserving function metadata.
    Shows practical use of decorators for fault tolerance.


Circuit Breaker Pattern:
    Implements the circuit breaker design pattern.
    Prevents continuous failing calls to an unstable service.
    Blocks execution after a maximum number of failures (e.g., 2).
    Logs WARNING for failures and ERROR when the circuit is open.
    Demonstrates reliability patterns in microservices.
    Protects system resources from repeated failure loops.
    Uses decorators to transparently wrap service calls.
    Good example of resilience in distributed systems.


Memoization or Expensive Function
    Demonstrates memoization using functools.lru_cache.
    Optimizes expensive recursive functions (like Fibonacci).
    Stores results of previous calls to avoid recalculations.
    Logs execution to show when the function is recomputed.
    Shows performance improvement for repeated computations.
    Example of decorators + caching in real-world scenarios.
    Great for expensive DB queries, API calls, or computations.
    Enhances efficiency in AI/ML tasks with heavy math.


Role Based Access:
    Implements a decorator for role-based access control.
    Only users with specific roles (e.g., admin) can perform actions.
    Logs INFO for allowed access and ERROR for denied access.
    Demonstrates how to secure sensitive functions in Python apps.
    Useful for authentication & authorization in web apps.
    Simple real-world application of decorators for security.
    Encapsulates access-checking logic without changing core code.
    Example: only admins can delete a record.


Track API Usage Count:
    Shows how to track API function call counts using decorators.
    Maintains a counter for how many times a function is called.
    Logs usage statistics dynamically on each call.
    seful for monitoring API usage in production apps.
    Helps in detecting unusual traffic patterns or abuse.
    Uses decorators for transparent tracking without modifying core logic.
    Great for analytics and system monitoring.
    Real-life use case in rate-limiting and logging APIs.